
<% content_for :title, "Let Creativity In | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="creative-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="creative-hero">

    <picture class="creative-background" aria-hidden="true">
      <source
        media="(min-width: 768px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('toolbox_let_creativity_in_desktop.webp') %>"
      >
      <%= image_tag "toolbox_let_creativity_in_mobile.webp",
          alt: "",
          loading: "eager" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="creative-hero-content">

      <p class="creative-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / LET CREATIVITY IN
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>LET</span>
        <span>CREATIVITY</span>
        <span>IN.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="creative-tagline">
        PERFECTION CAN PISS OFF.
      </p>

      <p class="creative-intro">
        MAKE SOMETHING BECAUSE YOU WANT TO.
        NOT BECAUSE IT HAS TO ACCOMPLISH ANYTHING.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / MAKE SOME ROOM -->
  <section class="creative-section creative-pink">
    <div class="creative-inner">

      <p class="creative-number">01 / MAKE SOME ROOM</p>

      <h2>
        YOU GET TO<br>
        MAKE THINGS.
      </h2>

      <div class="creative-copy">

        <p>
          You spend enough time managing
          your mood, sobriety, pain,
          responsibilities, and everything
          else that requires attention.
          Sometimes you need an activity
          that exists simply because
          you enjoy doing it.
        </p>

        <p>
          You like working with your hands,
          experimenting with materials,
          and turning ideas into something
          tangible. Glass engraving,
          drawing, crafts, website design,
          or whatever catches your interest.
        </p>

        <p>
          Creativity gives you something
          different to focus on. Instead
          of spending the afternoon
          replaying conversations or
          worrying about the future,
          you can make something.
        </p>

        <p>
          It doesn't have to become
          a product, a business,
          or an accomplishment.
          It doesn't even have
          to turn out well.
        </p>

        <p>
          You're allowed to create
          something for no reason
          beyond wanting to.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="creative-callout">
        <p>
          WHAT WOULD YOU LIKE TO MAKE
          IF YOU DIDN'T HAVE TO WORRY
          ABOUT WHETHER IT TURNED OUT WELL?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / DROP THE EXPECTATIONS -->
  <section class="creative-section creative-black">
    <div class="creative-inner">

      <p class="creative-number">02 / DROP THE EXPECTATIONS</p>

      <h2>
        IT DOESN'T<br>
        HAVE TO BE<br>
        A MASTERPIECE.
      </h2>

      <div class="creative-copy">

        <p>
          It's easy to imagine the finished
          project before you've started.
          You know exactly how you want
          it to look, and then every
          imperfect line or unexpected
          result becomes frustrating.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can care about quality
          without expecting perfection.
          You can want to improve
          without making every project
          a test of your abilities.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes the proportions
          are wrong, the colors don't
          work, or the material behaves
          differently than expected.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can repair it, change
          direction, start over,
          or leave it alone.
          None of those outcomes
          makes the time wasted.
        </p>

        <p>
          You're allowed to experiment,
          make mistakes, and produce
          something that isn't
          particularly impressive.
        </p>

        <p>
          The process can be enjoyable
          even when the result isn't.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="creative-grid">

        <div class="creative-box">
          <h3>WHAT SOUNDS FUN?</h3>

          <p>
            Think about what actually
            interests you right now.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maybe it's a new glass
            engraving design, new stencils
            for the woodburning pen, a
            handmade card, or
            experimenting with
            colors and patterns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose something because
            you want to try it,
            not because you think
            you should be doing it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="creative-box">
          <h3>WHAT'S GETTING IN THE WAY?</h3>

          <p>
            Are you worried about
            making mistakes?
            Do you think the
            project needs to
            be useful or sellable?
          </p>

          <p>
            Are you comparing
            your skills to someone
            else's or expecting
            yourself to master
            a technique immediately?
          </p>

          <p>
            Notice when expectations
            are making an enjoyable
            activity feel like
            another obligation.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / YOUR CAPACITY -->
  <section class="creative-section creative-yellow">
    <div class="creative-inner">

      <p class="creative-number">03 / YOUR CAPACITY</p>

      <h2>
        WORK WITH<br>
        WHAT YOU'VE GOT.
      </h2>

      <div class="creative-copy">

        <p>
          Your energy and physical capacity
          aren't the same every day.
          Depression can make creativity
          feel exhausting. Chronic pain
          can limit how long you're
          comfortable sitting or standing.
        </p>

        <p>
          You might have an elaborate
          project in mind without
          having the energy or
          mobility to complete it.
        </p>

        <p>
          That doesn't mean you
          have to abandon the idea.
          You can scale it down,
          work on one portion,
          or save it for another day.
        </p>

        <p>
          And sometimes you won't
          feel like creating anything.
          That's fine too.
          You don't have to force
          enjoyment or productivity.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="creative-grid">

        <div class="creative-box">
          <h3>LOW ENERGY.</h3>

          <p>
            Design a stencil
            or color something.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look through patterns,
            references, or artwork
            that interests you.
          </p>


         

          <p>
            You can also do
            nothing creative today.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="creative-box">
          <h3>A LITTLE MORE ROOM.</h3>

          <p>
            Transfer a design
            onto glass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work on a small
            engraving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make a handmade card
            or try an embroidery
            pattern.
          </p>

          <p>
            Experiment with a
            new technique or
            continue something
            you've already started.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work with cassette tapes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose a project
            you can comfortably
            stop and return to.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="creative-box">
          <h3>KEEP IT MANAGEABLE.</h3>

          <p>
            Set up your materials
            where you're physically
            comfortable.
          </p>


          <p>
            You can make progress
            in short sessions.
            You don't have to
            finish everything
            in one sitting.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / TRY SOMETHING -->
  <section class="creative-section creative-purple">
    <div class="creative-inner">

      <p class="creative-number">04 / TRY SOMETHING</p>

      <h2>
        GET YOUR<br>
        HANDS INTO IT.
      </h2>

      

 

        <div class="creative-box">
          <h3>GLASS &amp; PATTERNS.</h3>

          <p>
            Experiment with a
            new glass engraving
            pattern or technique.
          </p>

          <p>
            Try an animal portrait,
            decorative border,
            floral design,
            or geometric motif.
          </p>

          <p>
            Create a stencil
            for a future project.
          </p>

          <p>
            Admire how cool you look in
            your respirator.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="creative-box">
          <h3>YARN &amp; TEXTURE.</h3>

          <p>
            Try an embroidery
            pattern, experiment
            with different stitches,
            or create a small
            decorative piece.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work with texture,
            color combinations,
            and simple repetitive
            patterns.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't need
            to complete an
            entire project.
            Practice a technique
            and see whether
            you enjoy it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="creative-box">
          <h3>YOUR OWN WEIRD SHIT.</h3>

          <p>
            Paint tiny mushrooms
            or strawberries
            on your nails.
          </p>

          <p>
            Design a set of
            decorative keycaps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Experiment with
            bone pyrography,
            unusual materials,
            or a completely
            impractical idea.
          </p>

          <p>
            If it sounds
            interesting and
            you can do it, give
            it a try.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / CREATIVITY & MOOD -->
  <section class="creative-section creative-black">
    <div class="creative-inner">

      <p class="creative-number">05 / CREATIVITY &amp; MOOD</p>

      <h2>
        MAKE ROOM<br>
        FOR CREATIVITY.<br>
        AND REST.
      </h2>

      <div class="creative-copy">

        <p>
          Creativity can be enjoyable
          during all kinds of moods,
          but it's worth paying
          attention to how your
          mood affects your pace.
        </p>

        <p>
          During depression,
          activities you ordinarily
          enjoy might lose their
          appeal. You can try
          something small without
          forcing yourself to
          feel enthusiastic.
        </p>

        <p>
          During mania, everything
          might suddenly seem
          fascinating. You have
          more ideas than time,
          start multiple projects,
          and feel convinced
          that every idea
          needs immediate action.
        </p>

        <p>
          The excitement can be real,
          but it doesn't mean every
          project needs to begin
          today or every supply
          needs to be purchased.
        </p>

     

      </div>

      <div class="creative-grid">

        <div class="creative-box">
          <h3>NOTICE YOUR PACE.</h3>

          <p>
            Are you enjoying
            what you're doing,
            or do you feel
            compelled to keep going?
          </p>

          <p>
            Are you skipping meals,
            losing sleep, or
            spending impulsively
            on materials?
          </p>

          <p>
            Are you working
            on one project,
            or starting
            several because
            every new idea
            feels urgent?
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare your behavior
            with your usual baseline.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="creative-box">
          <h3>GIVE YOURSELF A STOPPING POINT.</h3>

          <p>
            Decide how much time
            and energy you can
            reasonably spend
            before starting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Take breaks,
            eat regularly,
            and protect your
            sleep schedule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Save new ideas
            instead of acting
            on them immediately.
          </p>

          <p>
            Delay unnecessary
            purchases, especially
            when your mood is
            unusually elevated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your project will
            still be there
            tomorrow.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="creative-callout">
        <p>
          A GOOD IDEA DOESN'T BECOME
          LESS VALUABLE BECAUSE YOU
          WAIT BEFORE ACTING ON IT.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / START SMALL -->
  <section class="creative-section creative-pink">
    <div class="creative-inner">

      <p class="creative-number">06 / START SMALL</p>

      <h2>
        PICK SOMETHING.<br>
        MAKE A START.
      </h2>

      <div class="creative-copy">

        <p>
          You don't need an ambitious
          project, an elaborate plan,
          or a particular result
          in mind.
        </p>

        <p>
          Choose something that
          interests you and fits
          your current energy
          and physical capacity.
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe you spend an hour
          engraving glass, do bone pyrography
          for ten minutes, practice
          a new technique, or
          go on the sewing machine.
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe you make something
          you love. Maybe it turns
          out completely differently
          than expected.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can change your mind,
          start over, leave it
          unfinished, or decide
          that the process was
          more enjoyable than
          the result.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to
          make something useful.
          You don't have to
          monetize every hobby.
          You don't need to
          be particularly good
          at something before
          you're allowed
          to enjoy it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            MAKE SOMETHING BECAUSE
            YOU WANT TO MAKE IT.
            LET YOURSELF EXPERIMENT.
            LET YOURSELF ENJOY IT.
            PERFECTION CAN PISS OFF.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="creative-callout">
        <p>
          WHAT SOUNDS INTERESTING
          RIGHT NOW? WHAT MATERIALS
          DO YOU ALREADY HAVE?
          WHAT'S ONE SMALL THING
          YOU COULD TRY TODAY?
        </p>
      </div>
<!-- BACK TO TOOLBOX -->
<nav class="index-home-nav" aria-label="Return to toolbox">
  <%= link_to "← BACK TO TOOLBOX",
      toolbox_path,
      class: "index-home-button" %>
</nav>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>
